oh  hello everyone um uh thanks for joining  our session  uh it's a pleasure to introduce dr  sergey rigo from johns hopkins  university as our next speaker  sergey completed a bachelor's of science  in human biology in 2006 and a phd in  biomedicine  in 2011 from pancho fabra university  under the guidance of dr  uh francesco sas after that uh he was a  postdoctoral fellow with marcus covert  at stanford university until 2005 when  sorry 2015 when he joined johns hopkins  university as a professor in the  department of molecular biology and  genetics  surgery is the recipient of several  academic awards including most recently  a career award from the national science  foundation and a research scholar award  from the american cancer society  sergey has been a pioneer in  understanding how the dynamics of  signaling networks  impact the behavior of single cells and  his work has been both informative  and inspirational for many scientists  including myself  i think i speak for everyone i say we're  really looking forward to your talk  all right so first of all i'd like to  thank the organizers for giving me the  opportunity  of presenting our work here today my lab  is interested in understanding  how cell signaling in individual cells  allows for coordination of collective  cell behaviors in tissues  over the last 50 years and through a  series of genetic and biochemical  approaches  researchers have identified most of the  components of mammalian signaling  networks  however if one tries to predict a  cellular outcome based on the topology  of one of these networks than boolean  logic  you would realize that is still highly  unreliable and very much cell type  dependent  so in order to address this issue we  have to be a little bit more  quantitative  and identify those nodes of regulation  that are more relevant in each case  but the problem between more  quantitative is that individual cells  even isogenic cells living in the same  environment can be extremely variable  and this is due to a variety of  extrinsic and extrinsic  and intrinsic factors where extrinsic  factors are other cellular processes  that are not necessarily synchronized  between cells in the population  and intrinsic factors have to do more  with the stochastic nature of molecular  interactions  now all these cell to cell variation  make it  such that bulk population assays are  highly misleading so the next question  then is how can we  be more quantitative but have single  cell readouts  and the way we like to approach these is  by using signaling biosensors these  are genetically encoded probes  fluorescent probes  that allow you to measure cell signaling  in individual cells by live imaging  there's a different varieties of these  kinds of biosensors there's naturally  occurring biosensors  like the ones i'm showing here these are  proteins that undergo  a change in localization for instance  upon a certain signaling stimulus  or a change in abundance again upon some  signaling stimulus  but they are naturally occurring  proteins so if we just tag  these proteins with a fluorescent probe  we can  measure signaling over time  in individual cells now besides  naturally occurring biosensors there's  also a variety of synthetic bias sensors  that allow you to measure  things like metabolites gtpa's activity  or even kinase activity as is the case  of these kinase translocation reporters  that work as follows  basically these are synthetically  engineered substrates that have a  fluorescent protein  and about a 50 amino acid peptide that  contains a docking site for a kinase of  interest an nls and then nes in the  context of two phosphorylation sites  so the way they work is when there is no  kinase activity  these two sites are not phosphorylated  and the nls  is stronger than the nes so the  fluorescent signal is mostly nuclear  but when the kinase is activated it will  bind the dock inside phosphorylate these  two sites  inhibit the nls enhance the nes  and you will end up with a net  translocation of the sensor from the  nucleus to the cytoplasm that is very  easy to measure by life cell  microscopy now the other interesting  thing about these sensors is that they  use  one color per kinase so we can  uh use different fluorescent uh colors  and multiplex more than one as this  as i'm showing here this cell line has  four different fluorescent proteins one  um  just dedicated to tag the nucleus for  segmentation and analysis  and three other fluorescent proteins  that allow you to measure irk  jnk and p38 activities in  single cells over time these are not  steels these are videos  where just by calculating their  nuclear cytoplasmic ratio in each cell  we can measure  the signaling activities in those cells  over time  so the next question is why do we want  to use  live cell imaging instead of using  fixed cell approaches like  immunofluorescence  and the answer to this question for me  is to fall  first because it enables measuring  temporal correlations  that means if we had two biosensors in  the same cell we could  know which one of the two pathways gets  activated first  with a relatively high temporal  resolution and in addition and this is  particularly important to us  it allows to compare the temporal  dynamics of different cells within a  tissue  but in addition we by doing these  experiments we have uh  found and others that inferring dynamics  from endpoint immunofluorescence  analysis  can be relatively misleading and this is  for example  the case of nf capability dynamics where  after  stimulation this in this case is uh with  tnf  the transcription factor oscillates in  and out of the nucleus  and this kind of oscillatory dynamics  has been observed also for other  pathways not just nfc like p53  irk and others and uh at the end what  that means  is that if we try to infer dynamics by  looking at endpoint immunofluorescence  it can also be  relatively misleading  so now i'd like to share with you three  different  uh stories from the lab where we  leverage  lifestyle imaging of signaling  biosensors to  address collective cell behavior in  tissues  first i'm going to talk about work by t  making in the lab  who had the following question how  oncogene expression in tissues  alters signaling dynamics in the cells  that express the oncogene but also in  the neighboring cells  and in addition how dynamics signaling  dynamics can inform  cancer phenotypes so the way he  went about these was he created a cell  line  um based on the human breast mammary  epithelial cell line mcf dna that is  non-transformed and chromosomally normal  where he could measure earth activity by  expression of the arc  ktr as you can see this is a very  dynamic  biosensor that again shows this  oscillatory  positive dynamics under basal conditions  now in addition to the irk ktr  tim introduced in this cell line a ted  on  system where he could induce the  expression of an oncogene and measure  dynamics in real time  then he tried about 15 to 20 different  oncogenes  and as a result he found that all  oncogenes can be  classified in two different classes one  class that  will increase the pulsatile dynamics  meaning  increase the frequency of the perk  activity pulses  in those cells and another set of  oncogenes that  increase the activity once and only once  and it remains sustained over time so we  call these  sustained oncogenes and pulsatile  oncogenes  for to differentiate them now in terms  of function  what he did was compare some of the  oncogenes that  created pulsatile earthquake activity  and some that  created sustained air activity and  measured different phenotypes  this was measured by edu incorporation  so what's the fraction of  uh cell cycling cells in the population  and he could see that all the oncogenes  that increase  cosital arc activity have also increased  cell cycle proliferation however  if you look at the genes that have  sustained perk activity  they have a very strong cell cycle  arrest we're now trying to investigate  the mechanism for these arrests but it  really resembles  the previously reported oncogene-induced  senescence  now we totally believe that these is  dynamics dependent and only dynamics  dependent for two reasons  first if you compare b-raf wild-type and  v-600e  these two kinases are exactly the same  they have the same substrates but they  differ by one permutations that renders  the v600e mutant uh hyperactive or  constitutively active  but also because if you induce these  kind of dynamics at different levels  as it's done here with egfr or mac2  you can trigger the same phenotypic  changes  increase proliferation when it's is  pulsatile  and decreased proliferation when air  conductivity is sustained  and in addition to these opposed  dynamics dependent sulfates  on cell cycle we can also see a change  in cell migration but the point of this  study was not only to look at cell  autonomous  phenotypes but look at what happens to  the neighboring cells  so what tim did was now label the  oncogene expressing cells with a  different fluorescent protein a green  fluorescent protein  and infused with doxycycline or not and  measured  the activity in the cells that express  the oncogenes  shown in green up here but also the  activity on the neighboring cells that  do not express the oncogene  and strikingly what he saw was that  certain oncogenes  which again were only those that trigger  sustained urc activity  not the ones that trigger pulsatile arc  activity  we're capable of triggering waves of  earth activity through the neighboring  cells and i'm going to show you here  an example of this striking finding  where there's three examples and you can  see the green cells activate but then  the neighboring cells have these waves  of  arc activity i'm gonna pause this and  play it again  these cells activate first and then the  neighboring cells  uh have a wave experience a wave of  earth activity  so that was interesting to us and we  tried to identify mechanistically what  is behind these waves  um we found out two things first that  they depend on egf  receptor signaling if one adds egf  receptor inhibitor you can  block the neighboring cell activity  while not  affecting the oncogenic induction of arc  activity  but also we found that it depends on the  matrix metalloprotease atom17 which  usually sits in the membrane and is  capable of shedding factors  such as egf like  molecules so what tin did was make a  knockout of atom17 this protease  and use those cells as  the oncogene expressing cells here  or the neighboring cells and what he  found is that if the neighboring cells  are out of 17 there is no difference  cells can still experience these waves  of activity  but if the oncogenic cells are knocked  out for the protease atom17  now the neighboring cells are not  experiencing erc signaling waves  so in conclusion what we think is  happening is that  sustained earth activity is somehow  decoded by the  matrix metallic protease atom 17  and once it's activated it can cleave  growth factor that then diffuses to the  neighboring cells and activates these  waves of erg signaling but what is the  function  of lateral signaling so since these  are these are epithelial cells and and  what we see is  egf receptor signaling one possibility  is that it triggers  proliferation of neighboring cells and  that's exactly what we see  so even though the oncogene expressing  cells  are arrested and suffered this oncogene  induced senescence like phenotype were  upon sustained earth activity they  reduce  the cell cycle fraction the neighboring  cells however this is a cool culture  with only 10  of roughly 600 e cells the neighboring  cells increase  proliferation by more than ten fold by  around tenfold  so this is very interesting to us  because it means that signaling dynamics  depending on the oncogene that those  cells accumulate  can dictate non-cell autonomous  contributions to  tissue growth and it's actually the one  thing cells that are that are growing  around it  but tim also realized something else and  when  we when when he was looking at these  movies he realized that  uh the neighboring cells have a very  striking directed migration  upon induction of the oncogene towards  the green cells and i'm going to show  this in this video  where um we induce with doxycycline  and the neighboring cells tend to all  clump around  um the green cells i'm just gonna go  back and forth in this video  and as you can see there is a very  striking directed migration  towards the green cells that he could  quantify by measuring the angle of  motion of  each cell towards the nearest  grain cell upon addition of doxycycline  there is a strong bias  and this bias depends again on egf  receptor signaling  and atom17 so in looking through the  literature and thinking about it  we thought that maybe this directed  migration of neighboring cells was  contributing to the so-called oncogenic  cell extrusion by which  cells are pushed oncogenic cells are  pushed out of the monolayer  so to address this question what he did  was using a confocal  um and plotting z projections with a  z-stack he could see that upon addition  of doxycycline the green cells are  clearly  up from the monolayer so they are being  extruded  we came up with a way of quantifying  this extrusion  again we could see that extrusion is  dynamics dependent  only oncogenes that have sustained air  connectivity  are capable of extruding and  also he found that these extrusion  depends  on the matrix metalloprotease out of 17.  so if there is  no lateral signaling um  extrusion does not happen so what we  believe is happening  is that depending on whether the  oncogene triggers  hostile or sustained earth signaling we  will have  two very different scenarios one in the  case of pulsatile arc signaling you will  have cell autonomous proliferation  and in the case of sustained erc  signaling you'll have a cell cycle  arrest but then due to activation of  atom17 and  and shedding of egf receptor  uh ligands in this case i'm for reguline  um the neighboring cells will now be the  ones proliferating  and the oncogenic cells will be extruded  from the monolayer now whether this  extrusion  is protective from oncogenic process  and development of malignant tumors  or it in it contributes to  metastasis we don't know yet  so now i'm going to change gears a  little bit i'm going to tell you about  some of helen's work so  helen is a graduate student in the lab  that had the following questions  how do epithelial tissues quantify  pathogen-associated molecular patterns  so what she did was similar to what tim  did  using life cell biosensors to measure nf  capability  signaling dynamics in epithelial tissues  again breast mammary epithelial cells  grown in a monolayer  and stimulated those cells with a large  variety  of pathogen-associated  molecular patterns and cytokines that  are known to trigger nf capability  activity  and measured both the translocation the  nuclear translocation  of nf kappa b this is heat maps where  each line is in  a single cell and each row is a time  and we have the localization and the  gene expression in tandem for each  cell so this is this summarizes about 18  000 cells in one experiment and what she  found from this screen  was that the bacterial lipopeptide pam  3 csk4 triggers a very all or non  response in in epithelial tissues she  verified these  using primary organoids and breast  organized and  got organized now this bimodal behavior  was very striking to us  and so what she did was try to stimulate  cells with other molecules that are not  necessarily in the initial screen  but that signal also through either tlr1  or two what she found is that all the  molecules she tried  that signal through tlr2 or  a dimer of tlr2 triggered this digital  behavior  and interestingly even though she  increased the dose by a thousand-fold  the response seems to be capped at  around  12 13 of cells this is variable between  experiments some  somewhat but it's pretty hard set at  um about between 10 to 15 of cells  so it seems like the upper limit of the  amount of responders  is somewhat limited at the  population level now since this was  all tlr2 dependent inputs what what  helen found out  is that the expression of tlr2 was  limiting so she  induced heterologous expression of tlr2  and could see that  now all cells become responders to these  lipopeptides and in addition she used  single molecule  mrna fish and found that  the responder cells are um  significantly expressing more tlr2 in  fact  the non-responder cells are almost  non-expressers  so how is this tlr2 expression being  regulated was the next  question and what helen found out  through the literature is that there are  several papers that link epigenetic  regulation to tlr2 expression so she  tried a few epigenetic regulators  and found that when we inhibit dna  methylase  activity in cells we can increase the  fraction of responders dramatically  about 60 70  so we thought that maybe the expression  of tlr2 was  controlled by methylation so to in order  to  measure methylation in single cells we  couldn't necessarily do that  but what she did was use nanopore to do  it at a single molecule level  so when we do nanopore sequencing and we  can get methylation  reads we can see how the methylation of  the tlr2 promoter  is bimodal as well so methylation occurs  in block so either  all methylated or all unmethylated  and the fraction of unmethylated versus  methylated reads  maps very well the fraction of  responders and  non-responders in her functional assays  so then we did some modeling and we  realized that there is a certain balance  here where cells have a probability of  switching from responder to  non-responder and from non-responder to  responder  but more importantly helen uh showed  that  if you incubate these tissues with  other inflammatory molecules  these balances of switching from  responder to non-responder or the other  way around  can be modulated and when that happens  the fraction of responders at the tissue  level  changes so what we believe is happening  is that the bimodal expression of tlr2  allows for a tissue level  response so at the at the tissue level  there is an epigenetically regulated  digital signaling that defines  innate immunity  now again i'm going to switch gears and  talk to you about my last example which  is  mike's work mike is also a grad student  in the lab and he had the following  question how  map kinase signaling dynamics patterns  the mammalian  pre-implantation embryo now  in mouse pre-implantation development  what happens is the first few divisions  are  generating um the same  type of cells and you you end up with a  morola that has about 16 to 32  cells which eventually um  develops a cavity inside separating what  we call the truffle ectoderm and the  inner cell mass  now the next step is inner cell mass  further segregates into lineages  one called the epiblast that it's marked  by nano  expression and one that is called the  primitive endoderm that's marked  by a gata6 expression now through the  literature it is clear that earth  activity is involved in this segregation  because if you add fgf4 all  cells choose the primitive endoderm  phenotype  but if you add an erc inhibitor or do an  fgf4  knockout all the cells choose the  epiblast  fate so what mike set out to do  is can we actually express the irk ktr  in mouse and measure our  signaling dynamics while this  specification is taking place  so he first developed the mice and the  first thing that he realized  is that most of the cells of the inner  cell mass are actually  active on perk activity but based on the  genetic evidence  we would expect at least a fraction of  cells that are low on earth  activity so that was surprising  but again the beauty of this approach is  to do  live cell imaging so he used confocal  spinning disconfocal to acquire z-stacks  over time  and he made the following discovery  i'm gonna play a video here this is the  h2b signal this is the irk  ktr signal and i'm i'm going to point  out at a cell that has a very brief  pulse of perk inactivity  so while most cells are active all the  time  you'll see here there how  at some point for a brief period of  about two hours  some cells experience these deeps of er  inactivity um that were the only time  in this inner cell mass development  where we could see low significantly low  earth activity  so then he measured individual traces  of the of a few embryos and the striking  finding was that this  inhibition of perk activity only occurs  after mitosis at no other point during  the cell cycle  there is such an erc low activity again  these are heat maps where each row is a  single cell  and each column is a time the blue marks  mitotic events  and after every blue you have the two  daughter cells  and for example these is an irk what we  call an  arc low division and this would be an  erg  high division where cells resume with  higher activity after division  now to make it more clear we've uh now  done an silicon synchronization of many  of these events  and we can confirm that it's right after  mitosis  that cells experience an irk low  activity  or arc high so we think there is a  bifurcation of urc activity after  mitosis  that could explain um the segregation of  primitive endoderm and epiblast  in in the mammalian pre-implantation  embryo  but to prove that he had to go one step  further and  identify which is the fate of  each one of these cells so what he did  was doing the live imaging experiment  then fix the embryos use antibodies to  do an immunostain  and then re-image those embryos to  register  and identify which cells are which from  the live imaging  to the fixed experiment and  then being all those traces the ones  that are erklo divisions  and the ones that are irk high divisions  and quantify their levels of nano ganga  6  after the imaging experiment  and what he found was that in fact  if you plot the levels of nano and gata6  in each individual cell  and color the dots depending on the erka  activity  right after mitosis there is a striking  correlation where the cells that have  erk low activity after mitosis  tend to have higher nano expression but  the cells that have earth high  after mitosis tend to have got a six  expression  but if you correlate the activity with  uh the last time point just as a control  there is  no such correlation so we believe that  it is the activity of  uh irk after mitosis that helps  um tilt the balance of the nanogata6  expression  uh level and and favor  for the er glow cells nano activity  and therefore epic last um fate  again this would be the summary where  cells  can undergo one of two different fates  after mitosis we have the earth low  divisions and the erg high divisions  and those cells that experience inner  glow division  after mitosis they will  become epiblast they will increase nano  expression  while the er high cells become primitive  endoderm  and so as a conclusion it looks like  mitosis desensitizes  some cells stochastically to fgf  signaling which is present all  throughout this embryo to push the  nanogata6 equilibrium towards nano  so as a summary of everything um we  believe that the main advantages of  studying signaling with life cell bias  sensors are that they capture  uh very heterogeneous behaviors that you  can get very high temporal resolution  and also differentiate cell autonomous  versus  non-cell autonomous behaviors with this  i want to thank the whole lab i've  talked about  mike's team and helen's data  also our collaborators and our funding  sources  and i will take any questions  great um okay so we have  one question um what's the mechanism  underlying er  activity bifurcation upon mitotic exit  and manmalian embryos  yeah so this has been  part of our focus lately we were lucky  enough that the embryonic stem cells  this  bifurcation also occurs so it's there we  can do a lot more  editing and perturbations than  in the embryos and it turns out that  um what we think is happening is that  during mitosis the anaphase promoting  complex the apc  complex degrades the map3 kinase graph  that is responsible for transducing the  signal from fgf down to erc  so that happens stochastically for  different  periods of time and in those cells where  it happens  completely they become desensitized and  then they cannot  transmit the curved signal from the  receptor to the  kinase and therefore its activity is low  nano gets stabilized  and and the balance between nanogun  galaxies  gets shifted towards epiplast uh  awesome um  any more questions any more questions  well uh i guess not so i guess we can  uh close the q a and thank the speaker  for  an amazing talk thank you

